# Cisco One Experience Lab — local memory

dCloud **PseudoCo / Cisco One** lab. Client VPN must be up before SSH or playbooks. Topology lives in `Lab Topology/`. The single credential source for every playbook is vault-encrypted `Lab Topology/lab_access.yml`, unlocked by the repo-root gitignored `.vault`. The markdown lookup is an index of hosts and URLs only. Do not put passwords in playbooks or unencrypted inventory.

## Workflow

- Author playbooks on the student laptop (this Mac) in this repo. Do not write new playbooks on Kali.
- Commit/push only when the user asks. Remote: `https://github.com/imanassypov/cisco-one-experience-lab-automation` (`main`, public).
- **Collection `00_scriptserver_bootstrap` runs ON the script server**, over `ansible_connection: local`. The student SSHes to Kali, clones the repo there, runs `stage-script-server.sh` (base packages + `~/venv` from `ansible-automation/requirements.txt`), creates the repo-root `.vault` by hand, then runs `00_preflight` and `01_bootstrap_script_server`. There is no laptop venv and no SSH hop.
- **Every playbook after `00` runs on the same host** (campus EVPN `01`–`10`, and `02_`–`06_` when they exist): `cd ~/cisco-one-experience-lab-automation/ansible-automation/<collection>`, Ansible already on PATH from `~/venv`.
- Refresh the checkout with a plain `git pull`. **No playbook runs git.** `02_sync_from_git.yml` was deleted 2026-09-25 along with `roles/script_server_bootstrap/tasks/sync_repo.yml`: it only wrapped `ansible.builtin.git`, and its existence had encouraged putting the same pull inside the bootstrap, which ran from the checkout it was rewriting and after it had already read `requirements.txt` and the Galaxy pins off disk. Do not reintroduce either. The `lab.yml` seed and `.vault` checks live in `tasks/pod_files.yml`, which the bootstrap runs. Order is `git pull`, then `01_bootstrap_script_server.yml` if pins moved.
- `.vault` is created by hand on the script server and is never copied between machines. `00_preflight.yml` proves it decrypts before anything is changed. The shared dCloud passphrase is demo-only; production needs a unique vault password from a secret store. Do not put the password in playbooks.

## Script server

- Kali Linux at `198.18.134.12`. SSH user and password come from `lab_access.script_server` in `Lab Topology/lab_access.yml`. Bootstrap sets resolver to lab DNS `198.18.5.102` (fallback `198.18.128.1`) so `corp.pseudoco.com` names resolve.
- Collection `00` targets this host locally (`ansible_connection: local`, `ansible_python_interpreter: /usr/bin/python3` so the `apt` module keeps its `python3-apt` bindings). Only the sudo password is read from the vault.
- The checkout path is derived from `playbook_dir`, so the student can clone anywhere; `~/cisco-one-experience-lab-automation` is only a convention. Never create `~/cisco-one-automation`.

## Collections

- Playbooks live under `ansible-automation/`, numbered by lab-build order: `00_scriptserver_bootstrap`, `01_campus`, `02_data_center`, `03_dmz`, `04_remote_dc`, `05_sdwan`, `06_secure_access`, `07_assurance`.
- `07_assurance/splunk_evpn` streams the fabric's telemetry into Splunk. It is the only collection that SSHes to a host other than Kali (`splunk_server` → `198.18.5.109`), and it uses `paramiko`, not the default ssh transport.
- Inside each collection, playbooks are `00_`, `01_`, … in apply order.
- `01_campus` has two independent tracks: `sda/` (stub) and `evpn/` (Site 105).
- EVPN is **Catalyst Center GitOps**, not a `cisco.ios` underlay/overlay pusher. Fabric CLI is rendered by CatC from vendored Jinja DEFN/FUNC/FABRIC templates (`evpn/Catalyst Center Templates/`). The only playbook collection is `evpn/ansible/` (CICD `00`–`11`, verify `12`). Settings SSOT is `evpn/Settings/`. CatC API credentials come from `lab_access['Catalyst Center']`. Site 105 verify uses `lab_access[inventory_hostname]`.
- Live CatC is **3.1.5**. Ansible/SDK API profile is **3.1.3.0** (SDK has no `3.1.5` string). Pins: `cisco.catalystcenter` **2.11.0**, `cisco.dnac` 6.46.0, `catalystcentersdk` 3.1.3.0.1, `dnacentersdk` 2.10.6. Do not drop `cisco.catalystcenter` below 2.4.0. The bootstrap `files/requirements.yml` and the evpn `collections/requirements.yml` both say 2.11.0 and must stay identical; `collections/requirements-jumphost.yml` still says 2.10.2 and is the odd one out.
- `template_source: local`. `template_local_root` is `evpn/` (two dirnames up from `playbooks/`). Subfolder `Catalyst Center Templates/Site BGP EVPN Templates` → CatC project **Site-105**. Composite `BGP-EVPN-BUILD.j2`.
- `12_verify_intent.yml` = intent verification. Reads `DEFN-*.j2` bodies from **Catalyst Center Template Programmer** (not the repo — stage 06 seeds CatC from git/local, stage 12 must check what was deployed), collects live CLI via **Command Runner** (`POST /dna/intent/api/v1/network-device-poller/cli/read-request`, **max 5 commands per request**, task `progress` holds `fileId`, then `GET /dna/intent/api/v1/file/{id}`), regex-matches declared vs observed, writes `evidence/stage12-verification.md`, and fails at the end. No SSH — works with the VPN to `198.18.128.22–24` down. It does **not** build the fabric.
- `settings.json` matches live CatC hierarchy (state → city → building → MAIN). Four project rows: DC-Site-10 (WLC `198.18.5.103`, `C9800-WLC`, `HQ-Switching`), DC-Site-11 (empty, `Remote-DC-Switching`), Site-105 (`.1–.3`, `Site-105-Discovery`, no NETCONF, `BGP-EVPN-Switching`, Day-N `BGP-EVPN-BUILD.j2`), Site-106 (empty, `Branch-Switching`). DEFN hostname keys MUST match CatC `__device.hostname` (inventory name): `Site_105-Leaf1|Leaf2|Border-Spine.corp.pseudoco.com` (IOS hostname + `ip domain name`, not `.dcloud.cisco.com`). ASN 65535, VRFs Main/PROD/IOT. Border-Spine is SPINE+RR+BORDER; leaves CLIENT only.
- Source of the vendored trees: `imanassypov/CatalystCenter-BGP-EVPN-VXLAN` `6d2afa3a2becfe71f8f036ee79a0a1c164ba0433`.
- `00_` is implemented. `01_campus/evpn` has the CatC pipeline vendored. Other `01_`–`06_` tracks are stubs.

## Pipeline (as of 2026-09-02)

- Pruned to `00`–`10` on 2026-09-02. Deleted: the SWIM `06.x` series with `roles/swim` + `roles/http_image_server` + `image_servers` inventory; `deploy_yangsuite.yml` with `roles/yangsuite_docker` + `yangsuite_servers`; the config backup stage with `roles/backup_configs`, `config-backups/`, `group_vars/iosxe|nxos`, `inventory/cml.yml` and `platform_groups.yml`; and the verify preflight play. Survivors were renumbered to close the gaps. Stage numbers below use the new scheme.
- **SWIM was reintroduced 2026-09-19 as stage `09_swim`** (ported from `ansible-dnac-swim`), so the pipeline is now `00`–`12`: `08_provision_devices → 09_swim → 10_deploy_composite → 11_await_access_points → 12_verify_intent`. SWIM runs before the composite deploy so switches are on their intended image before EVPN/telemetry config lands.
- **`00_site_deploy.yml` imports all 12 stages as of 2026-09-24** (`import_playbook` × 12, `01`–`12`). Three caveats belong with any plain run: stage 09 reloads the fabric because `swim_activate` defaults **true** (`-e swim_activate=false` to skip), stage 11 blocks up to 10 minutes waiting for APs (`-e ap_expected_count=0` returns immediately), and stage 12 fails the run on any intent mismatch (`-e verify_fail_on_mismatch=false`).
- Inventory is `static_inventory.yml` only — one group, `catalyst_center` (localhost → CatC API), used by every stage. `campus_evpn`/`evpn_leaves`/`evpn_border_spine` were removed when intent verification moved to Command Runner, and `cisco.ios`/`ansible.netcommon` were stripped from the evpn requirements with them (the bootstrap still installs `cisco.ios` for ad-hoc work). Keep `ansible.utils` — `cisco.catalystcenter` action plugins need it. No CML, no `.env`.
- Ran on Kali: `01`–`05`, `06`–`09`, and the verify play. Do not re-run `08`/`09` against the live lab until asked.
- `01`: skip building/floor UPDATE (`site_hierarchy_update_existing: false`). `cisco.catalystcenter.buildings` PUT raises `UnboundLocalError: local variable 'func'`. Re-enable with `-e site_hierarchy_update_existing=true`. dCloud CatC already has the `settings.json` tree (four states → cities → DC-Site-10/11, Site-105/106 → MAIN). Verified on Kali 2026-09-15: `changed=0`, summary `Created 0, updated 0, skipped 16` with one `skipped  Global/…` line per path. That is success, not a no-op bug. CREATE still runs for any path missing from CatC.
- `03`: bind live Design names, do not CREATE duplicates. Live: CLI `CLI Admin`, SNMP `SNMPv2c Read`/`SNMPv2c Write`, NETCONF `defaultNetConfPort` (830), HTTP `HTTPS Read`/`HTTPS Write`. Each project row `assign_credentials.site_name` = that site’s MAIN path.
- `07` cannot change pods. Both the wireless design and the profile steps run `state: merged`, which only ever adds, so the SSID for the pod you left is never removed. Moving `lab_pod_id` 12 → 24 created `PSEUDOCO-POD24` in the design, left `PSEUDOCO-POD12` bound to `HQ-Wireless`, and the profile update 406'd with `NCND05054: PSEUDOCO-FLEX-Profile is already in use by PSEUDOCO-POD24` — one WLAN profile cannot serve two SSIDs. Reverted to 12 on 2026-09-03 (`8dc963e`); a stray unbound `PSEUDOCO-POD24` design SSID remains in CatC. A pod change needs a prune step: PUT the profile with only the declared `ssidDetails`, then delete the undeclared design SSID. Scope any prune by `policyProfileName` matching ours, not by name pattern — the dCloud pre-built SSID is literally `PSEUDOCO-POD#`. `network_profile_wireless_workflow_manager` `state: deleted` removes the whole profile, not one SSID.
- `08`: three passes. `POST /dna/intent/api/v1/sda/provisionDevices` cannot set wireless DeviceInfo (`NCWL10092`). `roles/provision_devices/tasks/provision_switches.yml` defers `family` containing `Wireless` to `provision_wireless.yml`, which provisions the WLC and sets its managed AP locations. `provision_accesspoints.yml` then names and site-assigns APs. Site-105 9300s provisioned 3/3. The WLC pass forces by default: `force_provisioning` comes from `force_wireless_provisioning` (role `defaults/main.yml`, default **true**), deliberately separate from the wired `force_reprovision` so forcing the controller does not also PUT-re-push the fabric switches. Forcing is the default because the module short-circuits on "already provisioned" without diffing intent, so a changed `managed_ap_locations` would otherwise never land. Disable with `-e force_wireless_provisioning=false`. Gates: `wireless_provision_enabled`, `ap_provision_enabled`.
- Every WLC provisioning write goes through `provision_workflow_manager` (`provision_wireless.yml` only — the AP pass no longer re-provisions the controller, since `198.18.5.103` is shared across pods and AP provision is what writes the tags). Direct provisioning REST calls are the wired `POST`/`PUT /sda/provisionDevices` in `provision_switches.yml` and the AP `POST /wirelessAccessPoints/provision` in `provision_accesspoints.yml`. The wired payloads share one classification, `_wireless_ips`, so `-e force_reprovision=true` cannot route a controller at the SDA API. Remaining `uri` calls in the wireless paths are read-only GETs (family lookup, `family=Unified AP` listing, site membership, RF profiles). Deferral matches `wireless_family_markers` (role `defaults/main.yml`) as substrings — `Unified AP` needs its own marker because an AP's family contains no "Wireless".
- **AP provisioning is a real, separate CatC action, and stage 08 does it (Step F of `provision_accesspoints.yml`, verified live 2026-09-15).** Provisioning the WLC creates the WLAN and policy profiles; the tags that bind an AP to those profiles are created only when the AP itself is provisioned. That is Step 34 "Provision the AP" of "Provision a Cisco Catalyst 9800 Series Wireless Controller", which points at "Provision Cisco APs on day 1" (Inventory → select APs → Actions → Provision → Provision Device → Assign Site to a **floor** → Configuration for AP zone / RF profile → Deploy). Guide: `m_provision-wireless-devices.html#id_91719`.
- That wizard's Assign Site step notes CatC does **not** set that site as the AP *location* during provisioning (separate Configure APs workflow) — which is why an AP's `network-device` record keeps `siteHierarchy: null` even after everything succeeds.
- Naming the AP, `assign_device_to_site` and a forced WLC re-provision are inventory membership plus a controller re-push. They are **not** the AP provision action and do not create the AP's policy/site tag — that was the gap. The earlier note claiming CatC cannot provision an AP was wrong.
- `rfProfileName` is **required** on the provision call. Without it CatC returns `NCWS14003` "RF Profile is required for default/empty AP Zone". This CatC ships LOW/TYPICAL/HIGH with **none** flagged `defaultRfProfile`, so there is nothing to inherit; the role resolves `access_points[].rf_profile` → the profile flagged default → `ap_default_rf_profile` (defaults `TYPICAL`).
- Idempotency comes from `GET /dna/intent/api/v1/wireless/accesspoint-configuration/summary?key=<Ethernet MAC>` and its `provisioningStatus` boolean. Provision only when false, or when `-e force_reprovision=true`. Re-running otherwise would bounce the AP.
- Verified end to end on 2026-09-03: `provisioningStatus` false → true, AP mode flipped to `FlexConnect`, and on the WLC `SITE-105-AP-1` moved off the three default tags onto `ST_Durha_Site-105_d97a1_0` / `PT_Durha_Site-_MAIN_70ab7` / `TYPICAL` (source Static), with that policy tag mapping `PSEUDOCO-FLEX-Profile` — the WLAN carrying `PSEUDOCO-POD12`. A second run reported "already provisioned" and changed nothing.
- Module roles in the AP pass: `accesspoint_workflow_manager` for **name/location/mode only, never with a `site` key**; `assign_device_to_site` (as stage 05 does) for the floor assignment; raw `uri` for the provision. `provision_workflow_manager` is not an option for an AP — it types by family and maps only `Wireless Controller`/`Switches and Hubs`/`Routers`, so `Unified AP` falls through to `device_type = None`, and the module has no `ap_provision`/`wirelessAccessPoints` reference at all (its `skip_ap_provision` is a flag on the **controller's** payload). `accesspoint_location_workflow_manager` is floor-map X/Y/Z only.
- **`accesspoint_workflow_manager` cannot replace the REST call, and this is NOT version-dependent — do not re-litigate.** Raised upstream 2026-09-15: https://github.com/cisco-en-programmability/catalystcenter-ansible/issues/71 — track that issue; if it is fixed, Steps D–F can collapse into the module. Checked 2026-09-15: the module is **5965 lines and byte-identical in 2.9.0, 2.11.0 and 2.12.0**, same line numbers throughout — `verify_wlc_provision` called at 3718, `function="get_device_info"` at 3993, `if site_required_changes:` at 2317, `wlc_provision_status != "success"` at 2318. Its `access_point_provision_new()` (4078) builds the correct call, but is only reachable via the `site` key that arms the SDA gate. The 2.11.0 bump fixed `wireless_access_points_provision` only. The gate is architectural: the module assumes SDA fabric wireless, this lab is non-fabric BGP EVPN, and there is no flag to bypass it. Re-check on a new release with `grep -n "check_return_status" .../accesspoint_workflow_manager.py` near line 4010 — if it is gone from the `except`, the module becomes viable. Inspect without installing: `ansible-galaxy collection download cisco.catalystcenter:<ver> -p /tmp/x && tar xzf /tmp/x/cisco-catalystcenter-<ver>.tar.gz plugins/modules/accesspoint_workflow_manager.py`.
- **Stage 08 provisions APs with a raw `ansible.builtin.uri` POST to `/dna/intent/api/v1/wirelessAccessPoints/provision`, not with a collection module.** Body is `{siteId, rfProfileName, networkDevices:[{deviceId}]}`; the returned `taskId` is polled and the stage fails on `isError`. Do not reintroduce `wireless_access_points_provision`. The old 2.11.0 pin was never in effect: `00_scriptserver_bootstrap` pinned **2.10.2**, and `ansible-galaxy collection install` **without `--force` silently skips an already-present collection**, so the bump never applied. Both were fixed 2026-09-15 (all three requirements files at 2.11.0, `--force` plus retries added). Keep the bootstrap pin and the evpn `requirements.yml` pin identical.
- The pin matters because the collection/SDK pairing is version-sensitive. In `catalystcentersdk` 3.1.3.0.1 (all three profiles), `ap_provision` → `POST /dna/intent/api/v1/wirelessAccessPoints/provision` (kwargs `siteId`/`networkDevices`), while `ap_provision_connectivity` → `POST /dna/intent/api/v1/wireless/ap-provision`, whose validator schema is an **array** of `{deviceName, rfProfile, siteNameHierarchy, type}` and whose signature is `(headers, payload, ...)`. Collections call `func(**params)` with no reshaping, so a module wired to the wrong one sends the options as query params against an empty `[]` body.
- `cisco.catalystcenter` 2.9.0 and 2.10.2, and `cisco.dnac` at both 6.46.0 and 6.51.0, wire `wireless_access_points_provision` to `ap_provision_connectivity` — broken, and it reports `ok`/`changed=false` while provisioning nothing. Only `cisco.catalystcenter` 2.11.0 wires it to `ap_provision`. Because that is a single-version window with a silent failure mode, stage 08 no longer uses the module at all. Every AP-provision conclusion recorded before 2026-09-15 — including the Visibility-and-Control / CFS-preview theory — was drawn from runs where this no-op was in effect and must not be trusted.
- `wireless_provision_access_point` is the old API and cannot substitute: its argspec declares `payload` as a list, but the `ap_provision` it calls runs `check_type(payload, dict)`.
- The SDA gate, verified live 2026-09-15: `GET /dna/intent/api/v1/business/sda/device?deviceManagementIpAddress=198.18.5.103` → **HTTP 400**, `status=failed`, "This device does not have any fabric role assigned to it". `verify_wlc_provision` catches that and calls `check_return_status()`, which fails the whole module. That is the single reason `site` is stripped from the naming config. Swapping the endpoint does not help: `GET /dna/intent/api/v1/sda/provisionDevices?networkDeviceId=<wlc>` (what `provision_workflow_manager` uses) returns HTTP 200 with an **empty** `response` for the same non-fabric WLC, so no SDA-family endpoint reports it as provisioned.
- An AP's `network-device` record keeps `siteHierarchy: null` even once assigned. Read membership from the site side: `GET /dna/intent/api/v1/site-member/{siteId}/member?memberType=networkdevice`.
- AP MAC gotcha: CatC indexes an AP by **radio** MAC (`macAddress`), not Ethernet (`apEthernetMacAddress`). `?macAddress=<eth>` returns 0 devices and the module resolves APs through that same query. `lab_ap_macs` holds the Ethernet MAC (what the student can read); `provision_accesspoints.yml` lists `family=Unified AP` and translates. Site-105-AP-1 as of 2026-09-15: eth `08:4f:a9:50:f0:28` → radio `10:b3:d6:6c:c8:60`, mgmt `10.10.255.101`.
- `lab.yml` (`lab_pod_id`, `lab_ap_macs`) is pod-local and **gitignored** since 2026-09-16; `lab.yml.example` is the tracked template and `pod_files.yml` seeds it with `force: no`. Before that it was tracked, which made every student checkout permanently dirty and made `ansible.builtin.git` (default `force: no`) refuse to update — so the bootstrap (which pulled back then) failed with "Local modifications exist" on every run after the first edit. Existing checkouts need a one-time migration: save `lab.yml`, `git checkout -- <path>`, pull, restore.
- Galaxy over the dCloud VPN is flaky (`Errno 101` / read timeouts that look like IPv6 problems but are not — IPv4 resolves first and `curl` succeeds). `collections.yml` retries 4×10s.
- Live WLC `198.18.5.103` as of 2026-09-15 (note: `lab_access['Cisco 9800-WLC']` resolves via `wlc.corp.pseudoco.com`; the `.102` in older notes is DNS/AD, not the WLC): WLAN 1 `DCLOUD-XAR-FLEX-Profile` SSID `PSEUDOCO-POD#` (dCloud pre-built, never renamed) and WLAN 17 `PSEUDOCO-FLEX-Profile` SSID `PSEUDOCO-POD12` (pushed by our stage 07). After a successful stage 08 AP pass, `show ap tag summary` reads `SITE-105-AP-1 084f.a950.f028 ST_Durha_Site-105_d97a1_0 PT_Durha_Site-_MAIN_70ab7 TYPICAL No Static` — Tag Source **Static**, three policy tags and three site tags. AP `location` is unreliable: it read `Leaf1` right after one run and `default location` after a later one, so do not treat it as a success signal — tags are.
- `10_deploy_composite` (was `09`) queues only `DayNTemplateNames` with `DeployTemplate: true`. Site-105 was flipped to `true` so the play would not assert-empty. CatC reported SUCCESS (`5d38a5da-561e-40fa-b00b-563ef5ab4eab`) for `172.30.255.1–3`.
- **A single invalid CLI line aborts most of the composite.** `telemetry enable statistics` is not valid under `l2vpn evpn` on 17.12.01 — CatC failed `FABRIC-NVE.j2` (5th of 11 members) on the leaves with `NCTP10214 … Invalid CLI`, and `ABORT_TARGET_ON_ERROR` then killed the 6 downstream members including telemetry. Moving it to `FABRIC-TELEMETRY-SPLUNK.j2` (the last member) only **relocated** the failure — CatC rejected it there too. **Removed outright 2026-09-19.** Two faults: the template emitted one line where Cisco's EVPN Route Statistics doc specifies two nested commands (`telemetry enable`, then `statistics`); and neither form exists on C9300 — `telemetry ?` under `config-evpn` returns `% Unrecognized command` on **both 26.01.02 and 17.12.01**, so it is a platform gap, not a release gate. Consequence: subscription 40113 (`/evpn-oper-data/evpn-stats`) reports no counters and the Splunk "EVPN Route Updates" panels stay empty; the subscription is left configured deliberately. The dead `DEFN-TELEMETRY-SPLUNK.j2` include was dropped from the NVE template at the same time. **Lesson: a vendor doc describing a feature does not mean this platform implements it — probe with `<keyword> ?` in the right sub-mode, which is read-only and settles it in one command.**
- Overlay **is** live as of 2026-09-03. Leaf1 `198.18.128.22`: VRFs Main/PROD/IOT with RDs `172.30.255.1:10|101|102` and Vlan10/101/102 + Vlan1010/1101/1102; NVE peers UP to `.2` and `.3` (L3CP for all three L3VNIs, L2CP 100010 to `.2`); EVPN BGP to RR `172.30.255.3` Established, 31 prefixes, 38 network entries. Came up Sep 2 14:36, so an earlier push landed it — the previous note that CatC reported SUCCESS while the CLI was absent (`% BGP not active`) was true at that time but is now stale. Still verify on the box; SUCCESS alone proves nothing.
- `maximum-paths ibgp 1` never appears in running-config (it is the IOS default), so the `{% if DEFN_NODE_ROLES[...] | length > 1 %}` guards in `FABRIC-EVPN.j2`/`FABRIC-OVERLAY.j2` only suppress a no-op line. `ip ssh version 2` was dropped from `FABRIC-NAC.j2` and is likewise absent from the box.

## SWIM (stage 09) — verified live 2026-09-19

- Use **`cisco.catalystcenter`**, not `cisco.dnac`, for SWIM only. `cisco.dnac` 6.46.0 `swim_workflow_manager.py:3378` does `tagging_details.get("device_tags")` with no default, so golden tagging dies with `TypeError: 'NoneType' object is not iterable` whenever `device_tags` is absent; `cisco.catalystcenter` 2.11.0 fixed it at 4280. Symptom of the bug: tagging reports success, `isTaggedGolden` stays false, and distribute/activate answer "No eligible devices" forever.
- **CCO import does not work in this pod.** CatC rejects every CCO image with `NCSW10375: … is not the latest or suggested image from cisco`, even builds its own catalogue flags `ciscoLatest: true`/`recommended: "CISCO"`, and `software.cisco.com` is HTTP 403 from Kali. Working path: `00_scriptserver_bootstrap` publishes `iosxe_images/*.bin` over nginx on **port 8080** (`/var/www/iosxe-images`, `--tags image_server`), and settings.json uses `"import_source": "remote"` + `"image_base_url": "http://198.18.134.12:8080"`. Images are gitignored and staged by hand like `.vault`/`lab.yml`.
- **The whole upgrade works end to end.** Site-105 `.1`–`.3` went 17.12.01 → **26.01.02**, install mode, `flash:packages.conf`, committed. Activation of 3 switches took **~16.4 min (984 s)**, inside the 1800 s `image_activation_timeout` default.
- **Activation looks dead for ~12 minutes.** Boot options are set at T+0, then nothing until the reload (`Last reload reason: Image Install`), then `install commit` ~4 min later, then tasks flip SUCCESS. Do not diagnose a hang before ~20 min, and **re-read the device** — stale pre-reload output is what made this look broken.
- During activation CatC shows devices `Unreachable` / `reachabilityFailureReason: "In Service Maintenace"` (CatC's typo) / `collectionStatus: Sync Disabled`. That is **maintenance mode, not a fault**; it clears on completion.
- `swim_workflow_manager` branches on CatC version. Our API profile is 3.1.3.0, so it always takes the **new** path: `bulk_update_images_on_network_devices` → `POST /dna/intent/api/v1/networkDeviceImages/activate/bulk`, whose payload is only `id` / `installedImages` / `compatibleFeatures` / `networkValidationIds`. **`device_upgrade_mode`, `activate_lower_image_version`, `distribute_if_needed` and `schedule_validate` are accepted by the argspec and then silently dropped.** Harmless for the upgrade (CatC picked install mode itself) but it means `activate_lower_image_version: true` may not actually be what permits the rollback downgrade — verify before trusting `09.6`.
- Debug per-device with `GET /dna/intent/api/v1/networkDeviceImageUpdates?parentId={taskId}` (records carry `type: DISTRIBUTE|ACTIVATE`, `status`, `startTime`, `endTime`) and `GET /dna/intent/api/v1/networkDeviceImages` (`networkDeviceUpdateStatus`, `goldenImages`, `installedImages`). The module never calls either — it polls the parent task via `get_tasks_by_id`, which does reach SUCCESS but shows no per-device detail. `networkDeviceImageUpdates` needs `order=DESC` **uppercase**; lowercase is HTTP 400.
- **Stage 09 re-runs are idempotent as of `4da67da`.** Once the fabric is on the golden image, 09.3 and 09.4 both return `"No eligible devices …"` as a *success*, but `"no eligible devices"` is in `swim_failure_signatures`, so the asserts used to fail a clean re-run. `roles/swim/tasks/image_state.yml` now GETs `/dna/intent/api/v1/networkDeviceImages` and passes only when at least one device is tagged golden for the image AND every such device is `DEVICE_UP_TO_DATE`. Only `"no eligible devices"` is forgiven — every other signature still fails even on an up-to-date fabric, and requiring `tagged > 0` keeps the silent-tagging-failure protection intact.
- **`goldenImages` is ABSENT from `networkDeviceImages` records, not null**, on devices the image is not tagged for (verified: 2 of 5 records have no such key). So `d.goldenImages` is AnsibleUndefined and `(d.goldenImages or [])` **raises** — which Ansible reports as an undefined-variable error pointing at the consuming `set_fact`, not at the `vars` entry that broke. Always `d.get('goldenImages') or []`. Build fixtures for this API from a captured real payload and render with `StrictUndefined`; a probe that uses `.get()` cannot distinguish a missing key from a null value and will give you a false-green test.
- These modules return `failed: false` with the real outcome buried in `msg`, and `failed_when: false` **overrides a correct failure verdict** — so asserting on `item.failed` after it is meaningless. The role compensates with `swim_failure_signatures` plus an authoritative `GET /dna/intent/api/v1/image/importation?imageName=…` after import.
- Three different "family" names, not interchangeable: `device_image_family_name` is the SWIM identifier for tagging (`Cisco Catalyst 9300 Switch` = 286315874, from `…/image/importation/device-family-identifiers`); `device_family_name`/`device_series_name` are inventory fields (`Switches and Hubs` / `Cisco Catalyst 9300 Series Switches`) used by distribute/activate.
- Rollback (`09.6`) and postcheck (`09.5`) are still **unproven on hardware**. Rollback stays gated behind `-e swim_rollback=true -e swim_rollback_confirm=YES -e swim_reload_ack=RELOAD_OK`.
- A tag on `include_tasks` selects the include, not its contents — every phase needs `apply: tags: [...]`, and anything a phase depends on (including playbook `pre_tasks` like `auth_token`/`load_settings`) needs `tags: [always]`. Without it a run is `ok=2, changed=0` and does nothing.

## Stage 08 "AAA CLI(s) are already present" — fixed 2026-09-22

- Symptom: `POST /sda/provisionDevices` fails per device with `AAA CLI(s) are already present on the device … Remove the CLIs, resync the device and retry.` Cause is a pod that was not reset cleanly: the switches carry a previous run's CatC AAA block while CatC holds **no** provisioning record (stage 08 says `Skipped: 0 already provisioned`). Stage 02 puts ISE `198.18.5.101` in `client_and_endpoint_aaa`, so provisioning wants to write AAA and the appliance refuses to overwrite AAA it does not own. This is an appliance-side check — **no `-e` override bypasses it**.
- Tell it is stale CatC config rather than lab base config: `pac key`, `automate-tester`, `dynamic-author client 198.18.5.101`, `dnac-client-radius-group`, `dnac-radius_198.18.5.101`.
- Fix, verified on `198.18.128.22/.23/.24`: remove in dependency order (accounting, dot1x/cts lists, `aaa server radius dynamic-author`, the group, `radius server dnac-radius_<ise>`, then the `radius-server attribute*`/`dead-criteria`/`deadtime`/`ip radius source-interface` lines) — **keep `aaa new-model`, `aaa authentication login default local` and `aaa authorization exec default local` or SSH login dies**. Then `write memory` (stage 09 reloads and an unsaved removal returns), force a resync with `PUT /dna/intent/api/v1/network-device/sync?forceSync=true` (body = list of device UUIDs, wait ~1 min for `collectionStatus: Managed`, CatC caches the old running config), and re-run stage 08 → `failed=0 changed=1`.

## Stage 12 verify_intent — Genie parsing traps, both fixed 2026-09-22

- Symptom of both: a **healthy** fabric reports `absent` / `no receiver` on every row. Green state is "All 51 evaluated check(s) match the declared intent", `failed=0`. Stage 12 is read-only, so re-run it freely while debugging.
- `filter_plugins/genie.py` ends with `json.loads(json.dumps(parsed))`, and JSON keys are always text — so Genie's integer keys (VLAN, VNI, subscription id) reach Jinja as `"40101"`. Look them up with `| string`, never `| int`. `overlay_vlans` already did this; the precedent was there and undocumented.
- CatC Command Runner returns the command itself as the first line of output. Most Genie parsers skip it, but the `show telemetry ietf subscription all` parser reads it as the whole output → `SchemaEmptyParserError` → the filter returns `{}` → every row reads `absent`. `genie_parse` now strips a leading line equal to the command. **The failure is silent**, because `SchemaEmptyParserError` is deliberately mapped to `{}` meaning "the device genuinely has nothing" — so a parser that suddenly sees nothing means: check the first line of the collected text.
- Do not debug this from an SSH capture. Command Runner text and SSH text differ, and the difference is the bug. Pull the real thing: `POST /dna/intent/api/v1/network-device-poller/cli/read-request` → poll the task → `progress.fileId` → `GET /dna/intent/api/v1/file/{fileId}` → `[0].commandResponses.SUCCESS[cmd]`.

## Assurance (`07_assurance/splunk_evpn`)

- Splunk host `198.18.5.109` — mgmt/REST `:8089`, web `:8000`. **Two different vault accounts**: `Splunk Enterprise` is the web/REST admin, `splunk_server` is the Linux SSH account. They are not interchangeable.
- **`sudo` on that host works non-interactively.** `inventory/group_vars/splunk_servers/vars.yml` sets `ansible_become_password` from the `splunk_server` vault entry, so read-only host checks run as an ad-hoc playbook with `become: true` from the collection's `ansible/` dir. Plain SSH as that user **cannot** read `/opt/splunk/etc/**` — those files are root-owned `0600`.
- Stage 08 verify runs five checks. The first three (`systemctl`, `ss` on 57444, collector `:8888/metrics`) test the **write** path and never run a search; the last two (`mstats … evpn_lookup`, `validate_studio.py`) are the only ones that **search**. Exactly those two failing while the first three pass means Splunk is refusing to search — the telemetry pipeline is healthy. Do **not** chase `cisco.node_id` vs `evpn_device_inventory.csv`; the evidence report's own guidance misleads here.
- **Known pod faults as of 2026-09-24, both escalations, neither caused by this repo.** Splunk is a licence *peer* of `dcloud-lm.splunk.show` with a mismatched `[general] pass4SymmKey`, so it has had no successful check-in since 2026-05-09, the grace period lapsed and `LocalSearch` is `DISABLED_DUE_TO_GRACE_PERIOD` — internal indexes and `inputlookup` still work, which is why the instance looks alive. Separately, KV Store is down because `/opt/splunk/etc/auth/mycerts/server.pem` expired 2026-07-22; mongod validates its certificate only at startup, so the deploy role's closing `splunk restart` merely *exposed* it. Fixing KV Store gains nothing while search is refused.
- Date a licence outage from `last_manager_contact_success_time` and from `index=_internal component=LMTracker "Signature mismatch" | timechart span=1h count`, never from `first failure time=` in the message — that restarts with splunkd.
- The collector is upstream `otelcol-contrib` with **no fork or patch**; `otel_package_version` 0.161.0 is a **floor, not a preference**, because earlier releases drop numeric YANG list keys and every per-VNI panel silently renders empty.
- Stage 05 lookups key on the **short** hostname. IOS-XE emits `Site_105-Leaf1` while DEFN intent and CatC use the FQDN, so the templates strip the domain for `hostname` and keep the FQDN in `source`. A mismatch makes every panel render correctly and return nothing.

